<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Rorke's Drift</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Rorke's Drift was fought on 22–23 January 1879 during the Anglo-Zulu War, when about 150 British soldiers defended a mission station on the Buffalo River against some 3,000–4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> warriors, hours after the disaster at Isandlwana. The garrison held, and eleven Victoria Crosses were awarded. The defence was celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and later in the film Zulu (1964).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rorkes-drift.jpg" alt="Battle of Rorke's Drift" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphonse de Neuville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alphonse_de_Neuville_-_The_defence_of_Rorke%27s_Drift_1879_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Rorke's Drift, called kwaJimu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, was a mission station of the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the former trading post of James Rorke, beside a ford on the Buffalo River, then the border between Natal and the Zulu Kingdom. On 9 January 1879 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a>'s No. 3 Column encamped there and on 11 January crossed into Zululand. The station became a supply depot and hospital, garrisoned by B Company of the 2nd Battalion, 24th Regiment under Lieutenant Gonville Bromhead. Chelmsford's column marched to Isandlwana on 20 January. After Major Henry Spalding left around noon on 22 January, Lieutenant John Chard of the Royal Engineers was in temporary command, and two survivors of Isandlwana arrived with news of the defeat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Chard, Bromhead and James Dalton of the Commissariat agreed that a column burdened with hospital patients would be overtaken in the open, and decided to stay. The garrison built a perimeter of mealie bags and biscuit boxes round the storehouse, hospital and stone kraal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> force of 3,000 to 4,000 men was a reserve that had not fought at Isandlwana. Under Prince Dabulamanzi kaMpande it acted against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>'s order not to carry the war over the border. Native horsemen and a Native Contingent company withdrew as it approached, leaving about 154 to 156 defenders.</p><p>The attack began at about 4:30 p.m. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulus</a> could not climb the wall and fought hand to hand at it. At 6:00 p.m. Chard abandoned the north wall, and the burning hospital was evacuated, with Privates John Williams, Alfred Hook and Robert and William Jones cutting through walls to bring patients out. By 10:00 p.m. the garrison held a small bastion round the storehouse. The attacks ended by 2:00 a.m., and only 900 of 20,000 rounds remained. The Zulus had gone by dawn, and Chelmsford's relief column arrived at about 8:00 a.m.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Seventeen British and colonial troops were killed and 351 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulus</a> lay dead on the field, though estimates of Zulu dead run to at least 500 or 600, including wounded killed after capture by the defenders and the relief force. Eleven Victoria Crosses were awarded, seven of them to the 2nd/24th Foot, with four Distinguished Conduct Medals, the most Victoria Crosses for a single battle. Some have interpreted the awards as a way to draw attention from the defeat at Isandlwana, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Sir Garnet Wolseley</a> thought them excessive, while other historians hold that the defence stands on its own merits. Elizabeth Butler and Alphonse de Neuville painted it, and the film Zulu of 1964 made it widely known.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Rorke's Drift” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
